\f0\fs40 \cf2 Cataract surgery removes the eye\'92s cloudy natural lens and replaces it with a clear artificial lens. A cataract happens when the natural lens becomes cloudy and causes blurred vision, glare, reduced contrast, faded colour perception, or difficulty with driving and reading.\
\
Modern cataract surgery is usually performed as an outpatient procedure. The eye is numbed, and most people remain awake but comfortable. A tiny incision is made, the cloudy lens is broken up and removed, and a new lens implant is placed inside the eye. The procedure itself is usually quick.\
\
After surgery, it is normal to have some blur, light sensitivity, scratchiness, or a foreign body sensation for a short time. Eye drops are usually prescribed for several weeks to reduce inflammation and lower the risk of infection. Vision often begins to improve within days, but healing continues over several weeks. Final glasses, if needed, are usually prescribed after the eye has stabilized.\
\
Most people do very well and notice brighter, clearer vision. Some still need glasses for certain tasks depending on the lens chosen and the health of the eye.\
\
Although cataract surgery is generally very successful, all surgery has risks. These can include infection, bleeding, swelling, retinal detachment, increased eye pressure, inflammation, lens implant problems, or less visual improvement than expected because of other eye disease.\
\
Seek urgent care after surgery if you develop marked pain, rapidly worsening vision, increasing redness, flashing lights, many new floaters, or a curtain-like shadow in your vision.}